            <ArticleTitle>Pain as an atypical presentation of squamous cell carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>263-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cancer of the skin may be difficult to diagnose clinically if there are minimal skin findings on examination.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To remind physicians that the symptoms of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) may precede physical signs of the tumor.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Perineural invasion of SCC was confirmed by histologic examination of surgical specimens.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We present two patients whose initial presentation of SCC was facial pain due to extensive perineural invasion. Their diagnoses were delayed until skin lesions eventually became evident months later.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Early detection can be lifesaving, thus emphasizing the importance of including skin cancer in the differential for facial pain or paresthesia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
